   `api-researcher`), one clear deliverable each.
4. **One owner per file.** Never point two teammates at the same file; use
   extra teammates as reviewers of a single owner's work.
5. **Zero idle teammates.** Shut each teammate down the moment its job is
   done (`teamctl shutdown <role>`). No lingering panes.
6. **Keep the toolchain current.** When teamctl or any provider CLI
   reports an available update, surface it to the user promptly (e.g.
   `teamctl update`) rather than silently ignoring it — and apply it only
   with the user's go-ahead.
7. **The user overrides everything.** The user's explicit instructions
   always take precedence over these rules.

## Command reference

    teamctl spawn <role> --provider claude|codex|grok [--model M] [--effort E] [--prompt TEXT]
    teamctl dispatch <role> --provider P --task "…"   # headless, watchable pane
    teamctl result <role> --wait                      # read the result back
    teamctl followup <role> --task "…"                # another turn, same session
    teamctl route <role> --task "…"                   # auto-pick best provider
    teamctl send <role> "…"                           # type into a live teammate
    teamctl list / teamctl usage / teamctl providers
    teamctl shutdown <role>                           # always, as soon as done

## Delegation posture

Read the user's posture from `teamctl config lead.delegation`
(ask / always / manual; missing or unreadable = ask). The per-prompt hook
also echoes the live value as `delegation=<value>`.

- **always** — delegate non-trivial work to teamctl teammates by default.
- **manual** — work single-agent; use teamctl only when the user asks.
- **ask** — the FIRST time non-trivial or code-executing work comes up in
  a session, ask once, plainly: "Want me to use teamctl agent teams for
  work like this (parallel teammates, can tap your other AI
  subscriptions — uses more, and different, resources), or stick to
  regular single-agent work?" Then offer to make it permanent: "I can
  remember this — say 'always', 'never' (manual), or I'll ask again next
  session." Persist an answer with
  `teamctl config lead.delegation always|manual` and confirm it back.
  One and done — never re-ask in the same session.

**Escalation valve (all postures, including manual):** when a task is
genuinely large — multi-file, parallelizable, long-running, or one that
would block this chat for a long time — you may suggest ONCE per session:
"This is big — want me to spin up a teamctl team for it?" Never nag; a no
is final for that session.

## Chat-based settings menu

When the user asks for the teamctl menu, settings, or preferences:

1. Run `teamctl config` and read the current dotted keys.
2. Present them to the user as a numbered menu in chat.
3. Apply each change with `teamctl config <dotted.key> <value>`
   (comma-separated values become lists), then re-run `teamctl config`
   and confirm the new value back to the user before moving on.

## Lead mode tiers

This identity is installed by `teamctl lead on` in three tiers: this skill
(discoverable), a marker-guarded CLAUDE.md block (always in context), and a
UserPromptSubmit hook (recommended — it re-injects the standing rule on
every prompt, so it keeps firing even after context compaction drops older
text). `teamctl lead status` shows the tiers; `teamctl lead off` removes
all three.
"""

# The hook reads the LIVE delegation posture at fire time (cheap direct
# grep of config.toml; missing config -> "ask"), so the lead knows the
